              heading: 'Part 5',
              text: 'Colours fade and disappear over time if they are exposed to excessive light, moisture and touch of various living organisms. This timeline shows important phases in Indian painting and the evolution of colour over time. NATURE’S PALETTE 10,000 BCE 3000 BCE BCE Birth of the Buddha Birth of Jesus 1000 CE 1500 CE Cave Paintings Harappan Painted Pottery Ajanta Murals Miniature Traditions 16_Visual Arts.indd 16_Visual Arts.indd 12-04-2025 16:48:55 12-04-2025 16:48:55 Miniature paintings are of many styles and are generally small in size. In India, these were initially done on palm leaves . From the 12th century CE, artists began to use paper as the base for miniature paintings. Their themes included religious texts, mythology, nature, portraiture and court life. COLOUR SOURCE Bright red Sindhoor or vermillion Earthy red Geru or red clay Yellow Yellow stone or cow urine Green Malachite (copper carbonate) Blue Lapis lazuli/Indigo Black Carbon White Khariya or white clay The table below tells you about the source of some of the colours used in miniatures. These were processed and mixed with gum extracted from the Babul tree to act as a binder. The word ‘miniature’ originates from \' minium ’, a red-coloured pigment that was used to draw outlines and write text in medieval manuscripts. DO YOU KNOW? Palm leaf manuscript Kishangarh miniature Mughal miniature Kriti 16_Visual Arts.indd 16_Visual Arts.indd 12-04-2025 16:48:56 12-04-2025 16:48:56 They used brushes made of soft hair from the fur or hair of animals such as camels, goats, cows, and hair from the tails of rats, squirrels and mongoose. Gold and silver were also used in miniature paintings.',
